syntax:
constexpr complex<float> operator""if(unsigned long long d) {
    return complex<float>{0.0f, static_cast<float>(d)};
}
// Implementation notes: This defines a user-defined literal operator for the suffix 'if'. It takes an unsigned long long integer as input and returns a complex<float> with real part 0.0f and imaginary part cast from d. The constexpr keyword ensures compile-time evaluation if d is a constant expression. Overloads for other types (e.g., long double) allow flexibility, but the compiler selects based on the literal's type. At compile-time, the compiler verifies that all operations (e.g., static_cast) are valid in a constexpr context, potentially optimizing away the function entirely.

code_example:
#include <complex>
#include <iostream>

constexpr std::complex<float> operator""if(unsigned long long d) {
    return std::complex<float>{0.0f, static_cast<float>(d)};
}

int main() {
    constexpr auto c = 4if;  // Compile-time evaluation
    std::cout << c.real() << " + " << c.imag() << "i" << std::endl;  // Outputs: 0 + 4i
    return 0;
}
